{"kind":"assert","text":"Supplement to changes-requested review 3371c70c on exact head 5f225115: the inherited HOME and XDG_CONFIG_HOME boundary has two additional surviving paths that the filed verdict did not name.\n\nHOME still selects executable configuration through a repository-local include. I reproduced this with the exact three constructor pins in a throwaway repository: local include.path was ~/attack.cfg; inherited HOME selected attack.cfg containing core.fsmonitor; git --no-optional-locks -C repo status exited 0, created the execution marker, and emitted the junk paths appended by Git. GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null closes the global file lookup but not tilde expansion inside an admitted local or worktree configuration scope. Therefore the current HOME regression proves only the ~/.gitconfig vector and the artifact claim is broader than its evidence.\n\nXDG_CONFIG_HOME still corrupts the status answer without using a configuration file. Git reads the default global ignore file at XDG_CONFIG_HOME/git/ignore even while GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL is /dev/null. I reproduced a baseline status of ?? c under the exact pins, then placed a matching pattern in that inherited XDG path; the same status command returned an empty answer. HOME has the documented fallback HOME/.config/git/ignore and needs the same treatment.\n\nThese are part of the same changes-requested security finding, not a new implementation assignment. The repair should neutralize inherited HOME and XDG_CONFIG_HOME for these commands, add real-status regressions for local include execution and default-ignore corruption, and describe local plus worktree configuration and their includes as the remaining explicit trust scope rather than one physical repository file. Keep the citation exit-code defense and the package-owned system/global pins. Do not merge this head."}